title: "ADR-0007: Use a managed event bus for order events"
summary: Order events move from direct HTTP callbacks to a managed event bus (AWS EventBridge), so new consumers can subscribe without changing the order service.
status: approved
owners: ["@platform", "@api-team"]
tags: [adr, architecture, events]
updated: 2026-09-18
---

:::decision{status=accepted date=2026-09-18 owner=@platform} Publish order events to AWS EventBridge
The order service publishes `order.created`, `order.paid` and `order.cancelled` to one EventBridge bus. Consumers subscribe with rules; the order service no longer calls them directly.
:::

## Context

The order service calls four downstream services over HTTP after every order. Each new consumer means a code change and a deploy of the order service, and one slow consumer delays checkout (p95 +320 ms in August).

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    O[Order service] -->|HTTP| E[Email]
    O -->|HTTP| A[Analytics]
    O -->|HTTP| L[Loyalty]
    O -->|HTTP| W[Warehouse]
```

## Options considered

| Option                                   | Pros                                               | Cons                                              |
| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| **EventBridge — chosen**                 | Managed, schema registry, per-rule retries and DLQ | AWS lock-in, 256 KB event limit                   |
| Kafka (MSK)                              | High throughput, replay                            | Cluster operations; overkill for ~40 events/s     |
| Keep HTTP callbacks + queue per consumer | Small change                                       | Still couples the order service to every consumer |

## Consequences

- :status[Positive]{color=green} New consumers need no order-service change; checkout latency no longer depends on consumers.
- :status[Negative]{color=red} Delivery is at-least-once, so every consumer must be idempotent.

:::risk{impact=medium likelihood=medium owner=@api-team status=open} Duplicate events cause double loyalty points
Consumers de-duplicate on `eventId` for 24 hours; loyalty adds a unique constraint on `(orderId, reason)`.
:::

:::decision{status=superseded date=2026-06-02 owner=@platform} Add a retry queue in front of each HTTP consumer
Superseded by this ADR.
:::

:::agent Rules this decision implies
- Publish events only through `events.publish()` in `libs/events`; never call consumers over HTTP.
- Every event has `eventId` (UUID v7), `type`, `occurredAt` and `version`; add fields, never rename or remove them.
- Consumers must be idempotent on `eventId`.
- Events must stay under 64 KB; send IDs, not full objects.
:::

## Discussion notes {agent=skip}

Architecture review on 2026-09-16 (attendees: platform, api-team, data). Data asked for replay; EventBridge archive covers 30 days, which is enough for their backfills. Kafka was discussed at length but nobody wanted to run it.
